 prediction markets converge on a four-to-five-point Donalds edge, though sparse and divergent polling keeps uncertainty elevated.", "watch": "New polls showing Jolly consolidating undecided and Black voters, or a late shift in the national environment, could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Ron DeSantis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's governor's race is an open-seat contest between Republican Byron Donalds and Democrat David Jolly, a former Republican congressman, with several minor-party candidates also on the ballot. The state's recent Republican lean, Donalds' fundraising advantage, and a consistent Lean R consensus among national race raters frame the Republican as the favorite heading into the final weeks.\n\nPoll averages place Donalds ahead by roughly four to five points, though available surveys are sparse and partly hypothetical, and recent releases differ: one university poll shows a double-digit lead while a Republican internal poll shows six. Prediction markets price Donalds' chances near 80 percent. Jolly's path runs through undecided voters, including Black Floridians he has publicly targeted, in a race lacking televised debates. The forecast calls the race Lean Republican with moderate uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1709, "margin": -5.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2654, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2923, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2636, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1983, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2414, "margin": -3.8}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2597, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2217, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2584, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.252, "margin": -4.3}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2745, "